
  [1mC. Event bus with pluggable subscribers[0m

  The logger emits records onto a bus; console and HTTP attach as independent subscribers.

  - [3mBuys:[0m genuine extensibility — a third destination later needs no core change.
  - [3mCosts:[0m it makes redaction a per-subscriber responsibility. A subscriber added in six months that forgets to project
    is a silent leak. That's precisely the failure mode the allowlist exists to make structurally impossible.
    Extensibility you don't need today, bought with the property you said you wanted.

  [1mRecommendation: A[0m, with one specific commitment — the core emits [3mtwo[0m values per event: a full record and an
  allowlist-projected record. The HTTP sink's function signature only ever receives the projected one, so leaking isn't
  a rule someone must remember, it's a thing the code cannot express. That single choice is most of why I prefer A over
  C.

  Does A look right, or would you rather I work up B or C?

